/**
 * @mainpage Software Rasterizer
 *
 * A 3D triangle rasterizer written from scratch in C++ — no OpenGL, no Vulkan,
 * no graphics library of any kind. The only drawing primitive in the whole
 * program is "write three bytes into an array".
 *
 * It is not trying to be fast. It is a **reference model**: an executable,
 * readable definition of what a rasterizer does, stage by stage, so that a
 * hardware implementation has something to be diffed against. That intent is
 * why several decisions here look over-careful — the byte-layout assertion on
 * RGB, the POD types with predictable flat layout, and the places where a float
 * will eventually have to become a fixed-point number.
 *
 * @section mainpage_pipeline The pipeline
 *
 * One frame, from file to pixels:
 *
 * 1. **Geometry in** — loadOBJ() parses `v` and `f` lines and fan-triangulates
 *    any polygon larger than a triangle. normalizationPass() measures the
 *    bounding box so the model can be centred and scaled to a known size.
 * 2. **Vertex normals** — face normals are accumulated onto shared vertices
 *    *unnormalised*, so each face contributes in proportion to its area
 *    (cross_Vec3()'s magnitude carries that for free), then normalised once.
 * 3. **Transform** — model, view and projection collapse into one Mat4 via
 *    multiply_matrices(). buildPerspectiveMatrix() copies `-z` into `w` so that
 *    perspectiveTransform() can do the divide the matrix cannot.
 * 4. **Rasterize** — drawTriangle() walks a bounding_box() and evaluates
 *    edge_function() three times per pixel centre. The same three values serve
 *    as the inside test, the depth weights and the colour weights.
 * 5. **Out** — writeFramebufferToPPM() writes the framebuffer as raw bytes,
 *    which is why RGB's layout is asserted rather than assumed.
 *
 * @section mainpage_layout Where to start reading
 *
 * The modules are a DAG, and reading them bottom-up is the fastest way in:
 *
 * | File | What lives there |
 * | ---- | ---------------- |
 * | vectors.h    | Vec3, Vec4 and pure vector arithmetic. Depends on nothing. |
 * | types.h      | RGB and screenVertex. Depends on nothing. |
 * | matrices.h   | Mat4, the transform builders, and the perspective divide. |
 * | model.h      | OBJ loading and bounding-box measurement. |
 * | framebuffer.h| Colour and depth buffers, the clears, and PPM output. |
 * | raster.h     | The edge function, bounding box, and drawTriangle(). |
 * | main.cpp     | Scene setup and the per-frame render loop. |
 *
 * raster.h deliberately does **not** include framebuffer.h: a caller needs to
 * know what a screenVertex is, not that a global framebuffer exists. raster.cpp
 * does include it, because the implementation writes to those globals.
 * Separating interface from binding is what would later let the rasterizer be
 * pointed at a caller-supplied buffer.
 *
 * @section mainpage_reading Reading these pages
 *
 * Every file, struct and function page carries the design notes from the
 * source, plus graphs generated from the code itself:
 *
 * - **Include graphs**, in both directions — what a file pulls in, and what
 *   depends on it.
 * - **Call and caller graphs** — the fan-out from the render loop down to the
 *   per-pixel inner loop is the same fan-out hardware has to pipeline.
 * - **Collaboration graphs** for each struct.
 *
 * Start from the Files tab, or from drawTriangle() if you want the heart of it
 * first.
 *
 * @section mainpage_more Elsewhere
 *
 * The repository README carries the visual walkthrough — rendered animations,
 * the flat-versus-Gouraud comparison, measured per-stage cost, the known gaps,
 * and build instructions. These pages are the API-level companion to it.
 */
